serious threat of acts of revenge or strong indications that their privacy will be seriously disturbed.

2. Without prejudice to the measures and procedural rules provided for in the Criminal Code, the Code of Criminal Procedure and other complementary legislation, once a person is constituted as an accused for the practice of the crime of domestic violence, the Public Prosecutor or the judge may, whenever the seriousness of the situation justifies it, within a maximum period of 72 hours, apply one of the following protection measures to the victim:

o a)- refer the victim of domestic violence provisionally to a temporary shelter space;

o b)- prohibit contact between the victim and the agent in places that involve proceedings in the presence of both, namely in court buildings and others;

o c)- determine psychosocial support for a period not exceeding six months, unless exceptional circumstances require its extension;

o d)- prohibit or restrict the presence of the perpetrator of the crime at the domicile or residence, at places of work, study and others regularly frequented by the victim;

o e)- seize weapons that the agent has in their possession, which remain in the custody of the authorities in the manner they deem pertinent;

o f)- prohibit the perpetrator from using and possessing firearms, notifying the competent authority for the necessary measures;

o g)- determine the return to the residence of anyone who has left it for reasons of personal safety, in the presence of the competent authority.

3. In cases where the agent lives in a common household, the injunction measure to be imposed on them is their removal from the residence, whenever such a measure appears necessary.

4. The provisions of the previous numbers do not prejudice the adoption of other solutions contained in special legislation on the protection of the victim's family members.

